# telegram.env — install as ${PF_CONFIG_DIR}/telegram.env
# (default /etc/private-finances/telegram.env), owned by root, mode 600.
#
# Read by one service in compose.yaml: telegram, the Telegram worker, after
# app.env. A key set here overrides the same key in app.env for the worker
# only. The file must exist even when empty: compose refuses to start a
# service whose env_file is missing.
#
# The bot token path, the group ID and the two members' Telegram user IDs are
# in app.env, because the dashboard uses them too. Until those are set the
# worker exits at start and compose restarts it in a loop; start only
# `db app` (see docs/first-install.md) until Telegram is configured.
# How to create the bot and find the IDs: docs/telegram-setup.md.

# ---- Automatic questions -----------------------------------------------------

# Optional, default off. Set to true to let the worker ask the members about
# unclassified outgoing payments by itself. Off, questions are sent only when a
# member asks for one on the dashboard. Replies are always proposals to review,
# never decisions.
#TELEGRAM_AUTO_QUESTIONS=true
# Optional; used only with TELEGRAM_AUTO_QUESTIONS=true. A UTC instant in exactly
# the form YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ. Payments booked from then on are asked about
# as they arrive; older ones stay in the app. Without it the worker falls back
# to at most five questions per member per day. A malformed value stops the
# worker at start, and so does an empty one: comment the line out instead.
#TELEGRAM_LIVE_QUESTIONS_FROM=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z

# ---- Reports -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional, default: reports are not sent. An ISO timestamp; household report
# snapshots created after it are posted to the group. Set it to "now" when you
# first connect the bot, so an old backlog is not sent.
#TELEGRAM_REPORTS_AFTER=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z

# ---- Automatic classification ------------------------------------------------

# Optional, default off. Set to true to let the worker file clear-cut expenses
# by itself and categorise receipt items (docs/adr/0004-automatic-clear-expenses.md).
# Needs OPENAI_API_KEY_FILE and OPENAI_MODEL in app.env. The manual
# categorize job in the worker service reads the same switch from sync.env.
#AUTO_CATEGORIZE_CLEAR_EXPENSES=true
# Optional. Path to a curated JSON file of earlier explanations, matched exactly
# before any paid model call (docs/historical-knowledge.md). A private file of
# your own; not a credential, but it describes your spending, so keep it in the
# configuration directory, owned by PF_UID, not in Git.
#HISTORICAL_KNOWLEDGE_FILE=/etc/private-finances/historical-knowledge.json
